  "slug": "contains-duplicate-ii",
  "title": "Contains Duplicate II",
  "topic": "sliding-windows",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "pattern": "hash set sliding window over the last k indices",
  "statement": "Readings from a sensor are recorded in order in `nums`. Two readings at indices `i` and `j` with `i < j` belong to the same burst when `nums[i] == nums[j]` and `j - i <= k`.\n\nReturn `true` if any pair of indices forms such a burst, and `false` otherwise.\n\nFor example, `nums = [1, 2, 3, 1]` with `k = 3` gives `true`, because indices 0 and 3 are exactly `k` apart.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 100`, `0 <= nums[i] <= 100`, and `1 <= k <= nums.length`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [0], k = 1",
      "output": "false",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [71], k = 1",
      "output": "false",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[], k: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Only readings close together matter, so there is no point remembering the whole array.",
    "Think of a window of the k most recent indices sliding over the array; a duplicate inside that window is enough to answer true.",
    "Check membership before inserting the current value, then drop nums[i - k] once i has reached k."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force compares every pair of indices directly, using the inner loop's break as soon as the gap exceeds k, so the cost is bounded by the number of pairs within distance k and is O(n\u00b2) in the worst case. The optimal maintains a hash set holding the values of the last k indices: at index i it first checks whether nums[i] is already present (which means a partner within distance k), then inserts it, then evicts nums[i - k] so the set always describes exactly the window of the previous k indices.",
    "why_optimal": "Each element is examined once and the window holds at most k values, giving O(n) time and O(k) extra space; because only positions within distance k can match, any correct method must at least keep the last k values (or compare pairs, which is quadratic), so n reads and k stored values is the floor for n = nums.length up to 100.",
    "pitfalls": "Evicting before the membership check, which deletes exactly the partner sitting k positions back. Off-by-one in the eviction guard: the outgoing index is i - k, so it must be removed once i >= k, not i > k. Treating k as the count of elements a window may hold including the current one, which shrinks the effective distance by one."
  },
